      "clark_perspective": "Clark situates Hale's 1662 ruling within the intellectual history of English witchcraft prosecution: Hale's use of biblical authority (the text 'thou shalt not suffer a witch to live') and natural philosophical arguments in support of witchcraft's reality demonstrates the continuing intellectual plausibility of prosecution in educated English culture in the 1660s.",
      "hutton_perspective": "Hutton notes that the Cullender-Denny case conformed to the standard English social template: elderly widowed women with neighborhood reputations for cursing, accused after misfortunes befell neighbors following quarrels. Hale's verdict demonstrates that the social mechanics of English prosecution remained operative in the Restoration period.",
