    "scholarly_analysis": {
      "clark_perspective": "Clark situates Loudun within the demonological tradition of possession theology: the exorcists' procedures assumed that demons within the nuns could be compelled to testify against Grandier, treating possession as a form of supernatural prosecution, coherent within its theological framework.",
      "hutton_perspective": "Hutton emphasizes the political dimension: Grandier's execution was facilitated by his powerful enemies including Richelieu, and the possession performances served multiple social functions—institutional control of the convent, political neutralization of a troublesome priest, Catholic counter-reformation demonstration of supernatural reality.",
      "ginzburg_perspective": "From a microhistorical perspective, the Loudun case raises questions about agency and performance: the possessed nuns occupied an unusual social space in which diabolical possession provided a form of power and celebrity, complicating simple narratives of victimhood."
    },
    "legacy_and_historiographical_importance": "Loudun has generated a rich historiography and cultural legacy. Aldous Huxley's The Devils of Loudun (1952) is the most famous literary treatment; it was adapted into Ken Russell's film The Devils (1971). Historiographically, Robert Rapley's A Case of Witchcraft (1998) and Michel de Certeau's La Possession de Loudun (1970; trans. 2000) provide major scholarly analyses. The case is frequently cited in gender history of possession and as a case study in the intersection of political and religious persecution.",
