    "biography": "Dame Alice Kyteler was one of the wealthiest and most prominent residents of medieval Kilkenny, a widow four times over who had accumulated substantial property through her marriages and her moneylending business. She was of Anglo-Norman background, part of the ruling colonial elite in medieval Ireland, and her wealth and social connections gave her considerably more resources to resist prosecution than most accused. When Bishop Richard de Ledrede of Ossory launched his prosecution against her household in 1324, Kyteler was able to deploy those resources to escape.\n\nDe Ledrede was a Franciscan who had been educated in France and brought with him the continental tradition of heresy prosecution combined with recent developments in demonological thinking. His charges against Kyteler and her household were the most elaborate and theologically sophisticated witch charges yet brought in the British Isles: they accused her of maintaining a demonic incubus named Robin Artisson as a familiar and lover, of killing her successive husbands through poisoning and magic, of denying the Christian faith, and of leading a heretical sect among her household members. These charges drew on the latest continental developments in heresy prosecution.\n\nKyteler used her legal and social connections to frustrate de Ledrede's efforts. She had her son William Outlawe — himself accused — appeal to the Irish secular authorities, who imprisoned the Bishop temporarily. She apparently fled to England before she could be apprehended, and was never captured. The prosecution continued in her absence, falling most heavily on her maidservant Petronilla de Meath, who was flogged and burned. Kyteler's own fate after her flight is unknown — she disappears from the historical record.",
    "arrest_narrative": "De Ledrede attempted to arrest Kyteler in 1324 on charges of heresy and sorcery but was frustrated by her legal maneuvers and her connection to secular Irish authorities. She fled to England before capture could be effected, escaping the prosecution entirely while her servant Petronilla bore the punishment.",
