    "biography": "Alison Peirson of Boarhills in Fife was a healer who claimed her knowledge came from instruction by fairy beings, and whose reputation was sufficient to attract the attention of Patrick Adamson, the Archbishop of St Andrews, one of the most powerful ecclesiastical figures in Scotland. She was said to have treated Adamson for illness using her fairy-derived skills. This connection to a prominent churchman lent her case unusual visibility and may have complicated efforts by any powerful protector to shield her from prosecution.\n\nLike Bessie Dunlop before her and other Scottish cunning women of the period, Peirson situated her healing knowledge within a framework of fairy contacts rather than demonic pact. She described receiving instruction from the fairy folk and being able to move between the human and fairy worlds to obtain cures. This framework, common in Scottish popular belief, was by 1588 increasingly likely to be interpreted by ecclesiastical and secular authorities as evidence of diabolism rather than tolerated as harmless folk practice.\n\nPeirson's case falls in the period immediately before the great North Berwick panic of 1590, and represents the pattern of individual cunning-woman prosecutions that characterized Scottish witch-hunting before the mass panics. Her treatment of Archbishop Adamson raises the intriguing question of whether powerful clients provided any protection, or whether such connections ultimately increased vulnerability by associating healers with theologically problematic claims.",
