    "biography": "Ann Putnam Jr. was twelve years old in 1692 and was one of the most prolific and effective of the Salem accusers rather than an accused. She was the daughter of Thomas Putnam and Ann Putnam Sr., members of one of Salem Village's most prominent and litigious families, and her accusations were instrumental in the conviction of dozens of people. She named more accused witches than almost any other afflicted person, including the remarkable accusation of George Burroughs as king of the witches.\n\nIn 1706, fourteen years after the crisis, Ann Putnam Jr. made a public confession and apology in Salem Village church — the only one of the principal accusers to do so publicly. Read aloud by the minister at her request (she was too overwhelmed to speak it herself), the statement said that she had been a sad instrument for accusing several persons of a grievous crime of witchcraft, though she would not own that she had done it out of any anger, malice, or ill will, but by a great delusion of Satan. This public apology, which she sought and made, sets her apart from the other accusers and has generated extensive scholarly discussion about her understanding of what she had done.",
