    "biography": "Anne Gunter was a young woman from North Moreton in Berkshire whose father Brian Gunter, a local gentleman, orchestrated an elaborate performance of diabolical possession and affliction that he used to prosecute three women he had quarreled with over a football game gone fatally wrong. Brian Gunter coached his daughter to simulate fits, vomit pins, and display the signs of bewitchment, then used her performances to accuse Elizabeth Gregory, Agnes and Mary Pepwell of causing her condition through witchcraft.\n\nThe three accused women were tried and acquitted at the Oxford assizes in 1604. Brian Gunter, dissatisfied with this outcome, then brought Anne to the attention of the newly crowned James I, hoping that the king's well-known interest in witchcraft would advance the case. James, however, was sufficiently skeptical to direct that Anne be examined by the royal physicians and by Richard Bancroft, Archbishop of Canterbury. The investigation concluded that her symptoms were fabricated.\n\nAnne was brought before the Star Chamber along with her father. Under examination, she eventually admitted that her father had taught her to simulate the symptoms and had administered substances to make her vomit pins. Brian Gunter was imprisoned in the Tower. Anne herself was treated relatively leniently, apparently regarded as a victim of her father's manipulation. Her case represents one of the rare instances in English legal history where a witchcraft accusation was exposed as fraudulent during the prosecution process, and where the false accuser rather than the accused bore legal consequences.",
