    "biography": "Anne Whittle, universally known as Old Chattox, was an elderly woman who headed the second of the two rival families at the center of the Pendle prosecutions. She and Old Demdike (Elizabeth Southerns) represented competing local reputations for cunning craft and magical ability, and the hostility between the two families provided a social framework within which accusations flowed. Thomas Potts's account describes Chattox as a dangerous old fire, with a bleared eye, and emphasizes her fierce manner and apparent unrepentance.\n\nChattox had a history of entanglements with accusations of maleficium in the Pendle area predating the 1612 trials. She was accused specifically of killing Robert Nutter, and the Nutter family's grievances against her contributed to the strength of the prosecution case. Her reputation as a healer and a curser — typical of the cunning woman's dual role as both benefit and threat to neighbors — had made her a figure of both reliance and fear in the local community.",
