    "biography": "Barbara Napier was the wife of Archibald Douglas, an Edinburgh merchant, placing her solidly in the prosperous burgess class. Her relatively elevated social position, combined with the specific accusations against her, made her case one of the most politically charged episodes in the North Berwick trials. She was implicated through confessions extracted from other accused witches and charged with consulting Agnes Sampson and with participation in the North Berwick conspiracy.\n\nNapier's case took a dramatic turn when the jury acquitted her, producing an extraordinary royal reaction. James VI, who had personally invested in the North Berwick prosecutions as a direct attack on his person and his royal authority, was furious at the verdict. He threatened the jurors with a treason prosecution of their own — an astonishing exercise of royal pressure on the judicial process that illustrates how politicized the North Berwick trials had become.",
