    "biography": "Battista Monico was a Friulian peasant investigated by the Venetian Inquisition in the 1580s as one of the benandanti — those born with the caul who claimed to fight witches in spirit form during the Ember Days. His case is part of the second wave of benandante investigations that followed the pioneering 1575-1580 cases that Carlo Ginzburg analyzed in Night Battles. Monico claimed to battle witches for the protection of harvests using fennel stalks, describing the familiar benandante framework of ecstatic spiritual combat on behalf of the community.\n\nMonico's interrogation records show the characteristic dynamic Ginzburg identified: the peasant subject arriving with a coherent and confident self-understanding as a beneficent spirit-warrior, and the Inquisition's questioning working systematically to reframe this self-understanding within the heretical category of diabolical sabbath attendance. Monico initially described his activities in terms of community service — protecting crops, fighting against the witches who sought to destroy harvests — and showed little understanding of why the Inquisition regarded this as problematic.\n\nThe Venetian Inquisition's approach to the benandanti was relatively measured compared to the secular courts of northern Europe: it sought to investigate and correct rather than to execute, and many benandanti survived their encounters with the tribunal. Monico's survival reflects the institutional culture of the Venetian Inquisition, which was less inclined toward execution than many secular courts and more oriented toward penance and reconciliation.",
