    "biography": "Catharina Henot was the Postmistress of Cologne, one of the most prestigious civic offices held by a woman in early modern Germany, when she was arrested and executed for witchcraft in May 1627. Her family was prominent in Cologne's civic life; her brother Hartger was a painter and her family had connections to the city's professional elite. Her position as Postmistress placed her in a position of considerable trust and visibility, making her case particularly striking as evidence of how far the witch panic of the 1620s had penetrated the social fabric of Rhine-Meuse Germany.\n\nHenot's prosecution took place during one of the most intense periods of witch-hunting in German history, when the Cologne archdiocese and surrounding territories saw scores of executions. Her case attracted unusual attention partly because of her social prominence — the execution of a woman of her standing served as a warning that no social position offered protection. Contemporary commentary noted that her case alarmed many observers about the scope of the persecution, though this alarm did not slow the prosecutions.\n\nIn 2012, the city of Cologne formally rehabilitated Catharina Henot, acknowledging her as an innocent victim of persecution. This modern rehabilitation reflects both the scholarly consensus about the injustice of the prosecutions and the ongoing cultural work of processing the history of witch-hunting in Germany. Her case has become part of the political memory of Cologne.",
