    "biography": "Dorcas Good was approximately four or five years old when she was accused of witchcraft in March 1692, making her the youngest known accused person in the Salem crisis. She was the daughter of Sarah Good, who was herself among the first three accused and who would be executed in July 1692. Dorcas's nursing infant sibling reportedly died in prison. The accusation of a small child — who was said to have bitten the afflicted girls with her familiar's shape — is one of the most extreme illustrations of how the Salem crisis had departed from any rational judicial standard.\n\nDorcas was chained in prison, an appalling condition for a small child. Her father William Good later testified that she had suffered permanent psychological damage from the imprisonment — that she was never mentally well afterward. She was eventually released when the crisis collapsed and the prisoners were freed, but she survived as a permanently damaged person, a child whose mind had been broken by months in chains in colonial prison.",
