    "biography": "Dorothea Flock was the wife of a court official in Würzburg who was tried and executed during the latter phase of the Würzburg mass persecution in 1629. As the wife of a court official — a man employed in the administration of the Prince-Bishop's court — her social position was somewhat elevated above that of ordinary townspeople, and her prosecution illustrates how the Würzburg panic penetrated even the administrative households directly connected to the prosecuting authority.\n\nBy 1629 the Würzburg persecution had been running for several years and had already consumed hundreds of people across a wide social spectrum. The expansion of accusation into the households of court officials demonstrates the self-consuming character of mass persecution at its most extreme: the prosecution apparatus was implicating the very social networks of those who administered it. This dynamic would eventually contribute to the persistence of voices calling for restraint, as families of officials recognized their own vulnerability.\n\nFlock's case, like those of many Würzburg accused, is documented in the execution lists that survive from the persecution. These lists, which record names and social positions of the executed, provide the primary evidence for the social breadth of the Würzburg persecution and have been analyzed extensively by historians examining the sociology of mass witch-hunting.",
