    "biography": "Elizabeth Francis was one of the first accused in the Essex witch trials that began in 1566, and her case has the unusual feature of spanning three separate trials over more than a decade. At her first trial in 1566, she confessed to possessing a familiar spirit — a white spotted cat named Sathan — which she claimed had been given to her by her grandmother, Mother Eve. This early confession linked familiar spirit inheritance across generations, a pattern scholars have found significant for understanding how magical practice (or its reputation) could be transmitted within families.\n\nFrancis survived the 1566 trial with a lesser sentence and appeared again before the courts in 1572 and finally in 1579, when she was executed. Her long judicial history — three encounters with the assize courts over thirteen years — makes her one of the more individually documented English accused witches of the period. Her 1566 confession, describing how she gave the cat to Agnes Waterhouse, created a narrative link between the two women that shaped the entire first Chelmsford prosecution.",
