    "biography": "Elizabeth Southerns, universally known as Old Demdike, was an elderly, nearly blind woman who had been associated with magical practice in the Forest of Pendle for decades before the 1612 trials. Thomas Potts described her as a witch of fifty years standing — a characterization that, whatever its accuracy, reflects her long-established local reputation as a woman of cunning craft. She was the matriarch around whom the Demdike family network was organized, and her grandchildren, children, and neighbors were all drawn into the prosecution web.\n\nDemdike represents the archetype of the English cunning woman whose services to the local community — healing, finding lost goods, identifying bewitchment — had created a reputation that could be reframed as witchcraft under judicial pressure. She freely confessed during her examination before Justice Nowell in March 1612, describing a decades-long familiarity with a familiar spirit named Tibb. Whether this confession reflected genuine belief, rational calculation, or the confusion of an elderly infirm woman under pressure from an authority figure is a question scholars continue to debate.",
    "arrest_narrative": "Demdike was among the first arrested in the March 1612 examinations. Justice Nowell's examination at Read Hall drew out her confession without apparent torture — she was elderly, nearly blind, and reportedly submitted with relative ease to questioning. She was committed to Lancaster Castle, where the conditions were harsh enough that she died before trial, never facing formal legal proceedings.",
