    "biography": "Janet Breadheid was among the many individuals tried and executed during the Aberdeen witch panic of 1597, one of the most substantial Scottish witch prosecutions of the late sixteenth century. The Aberdeen trials resulted from a combination of local tensions and the national atmosphere created by James VI's active pursuit of witch prosecution following the North Berwick cases. At least twenty-four individuals were tried in Aberdeen during 1597, and a significant number were executed.\n\nThe details of Breadheid's specific accusations and confessions are less fully documented than those of her more famous co-accused Andro Man, who drew attention for his claims about the Queen of Elfhame. She was caught up in the same panic structure that swept Aberdeen in 1597, in which accusations spread through a community creating an expanding circle of suspects. The Aberdeen panic was driven partly by a general commission James VI had issued for witch-hunting in 1597, which gave local authorities the impetus to pursue cases they might otherwise have left alone.\n\nBreidheid's case illustrates the typical dynamics of the Scottish parish-level prosecution: individuals known to their community for unusual behavior or who had existing reputations for quarrelsomeness or strange practices became targets when prosecutorial pressure created both opportunity and incentive for accusation. The 1597 Aberdeen panic has been studied by historians as one of the clearest examples of how royal pressure filtered down to produce local mass prosecutions.",
