    "biography": "Joan Flower was an elderly woman from near Belvoir Castle in Lincolnshire who had been employed at the castle by Francis Manners, sixth Earl of Rutland, along with her daughters Margaret and Philippa. After her daughter Margaret was dismissed from service — apparently for pilfering or some misconduct — Joan Flower and her daughters were accused of having bewitched the Earl's family in revenge. Two of the Earl's sons died, and their deaths were attributed to the maleficium of the Flowers.\n\nJoan Flower was arrested along with her daughters Margaret and Philippa. According to the pamphlet account of the case, she declared her innocence by calling on God to show her guilt by making a piece of bread choke her if she were guilty — whereupon she swallowed the bread and choked to death. This remarkable death in custody before trial was interpreted by contemporaries as divine confirmation of guilt. Her daughters were subsequently tried, convicted, and hanged.\n\nThe Belvoir case is significant for the combination of a high-profile aristocratic family as victims (lending the prosecution enormous credibility and resources), the employment relationship as the origin of the dispute, and the unusual manner of Joan Flower's death. The case was sufficiently notable to generate a pamphlet — The Wonderful Discovery of the Witchcrafts of Margaret and Philip Flower (1619) — and a monument to the Earl's dead sons was erected in Bottesford church with an inscription noting they died by wicked practice and sorcery.",
