    "biography": "Joan Prentice was an elderly woman who, at the time of her accusation, was resident in an almshouse in Sible Hedingham, Essex — a position that reflects her poverty and marginality within local society. Like many Essex accused witches of the Elizabethan period, she existed at the social margins, dependent on community charity, and her accusation arose from neighborhood conflicts and suspicions that had built up over time.\n\nPrentice confessed to possessing a familiar spirit in the form of a ferret, which she named Bid. Her confession followed the well-established Essex pattern of animal familiars, neighborhood maleficium, and a pact with the devil in animal form. She was one of three women tried at the 1589 Chelmsford assizes, the third and final of the major sixteenth-century Chelmsford witch trials. Her confession, like those of her predecessors, was produced without recorded torture.",
