    "social_status": "Burgomaster of Bamberg; leading civic official",
    "gender": "Male",
    "biography": "Johannes Junius was the Burgomaster of Bamberg, the highest elected civic official of one of the most important cities in Franconia, when he was arrested and executed during the Bamberg mass prosecutions of 1626-1631 — the largest witch persecution in German history. His arrest in 1628 represented a moment of terrifying escalation: if the Burgomaster himself could be accused and executed, then truly no one was safe. Junius had previously participated in the machinery of witch prosecution as an official of the city; he now found himself its victim.\n\nJunius was subjected to multiple sessions of torture including strappado and leg screws before he confessed. His false confession named other members of the civic community as witches, feeding the ever-expanding circle of accusation. But what makes his case uniquely significant in the historical record is the letter he managed to smuggle from prison to his daughter Veronica. Written in circumstances of extreme danger — discovery would have brought further punishment — the letter is one of the most moving documents of the entire witch persecution.\n\nIn the letter, Junius describes being tortured until he confessed falsely, names the executioner who tortured him, describes each session in detail, and pleads with his daughter to work for his release while acknowledging he probably cannot survive. He writes: 'Innocent I have come into prison, innocent I must die... for whoever comes into the witch prison must become a witch or be tortured until he invents something out of his head.' The letter arrived too late; Junius was executed on August 6, 1628. The letter survived, preserved by his daughter, and was published centuries later, becoming one of the most powerful indictments of witch prosecution ever written.",
    "arrest_narrative": "Junius was arrested in 1628 as the Bamberg prosecution expanded to include civic leaders following the execution of his predecessor as Burgomaster. He was brought before the Bamberg witch commission and subjected to examination, then to torture when he initially refused to confess.",
