    "biography": "John Fian, whose real name was John Cunningham, was a schoolmaster at Saltpans (modern Prestonpans) in East Lothian at the time of the North Berwick trials. His position as an educated man gave him a social standing somewhat above most accused witches, and his role as a schoolmaster — responsible for instructing the young — made his alleged leadership of a witch conspiracy particularly alarming to contemporaries. He was named as the alleged ringleader of the North Berwick coven, the secretary and registrar of the devil.\n\nFian's case became notorious for the extraordinary torture applied to him and his dramatic recantation and re-arrest. Contemporary pamphlet accounts dwell at length on the methods used against him, depicting a man broken by suffering who nonetheless at one point managed to escape custody before being recaptured. His case illustrates the machinery of torture and confession in early modern Scottish witch trials with unusual clarity.",
    "arrest_narrative": "Fian was arrested as part of the North Berwick prosecutions and accused of being the principal secretary and schoolmaster of the witches' assembly. He was brought before James VI and subjected to systematic torture. After confessing under torture, he was placed in less severe confinement, at which point he recanted all confessions and denied any dealings with the devil. This recantation was interpreted as proof of renewed demonic assistance, and he was subjected to extreme torture a second time.",
