    "biography": "Margaret Barclay was the wife of Archibald Dein, a burgess of Irvine in Ayrshire, who became embroiled in witch prosecution through a personal dispute with her brother-in-law John Dein and his wife. The origins of her case lay in a quarrel with these relatives, after which she allegedly made a wax image of a ship and caused it to sink — with John Dein and the Provost of Irvine aboard — through sympathetic magic. Her case illustrates how personal disputes and community quarrels could provide the spark for witchcraft accusations that then took on a prosecutorial momentum of their own.\n\nThe evidence against Barclay included testimony about the clay or wax figure she had made, which witnesses claimed to have seen, and later confessions extracted under torture that described her meetings with a spirit or devil in the form of a black dog. She had accomplices in the alleged murder-by-magic, including John Reid, a pedlar who had helped make the image. The supposed evidence of the ship's wreck — an actual vessel did sink — provided the material grounding for charges that might otherwise have been harder to prosecute.\n\nHer case shows the classic trajectory of Scottish witch prosecution: a community dispute generates an accusation, the accusation triggers investigation and torture, torture produces a confession that names accomplices, and the expanding circle of accused draws in further individuals. John Reid, named as an accomplice, strangled himself in prison before trial, which contemporaries took as evidence of guilt. Margaret Barclay was strangled and burned in 1618.",
    "arrest_narrative": "Barclay was arrested following accusations that she had practiced maleficium against a ship carrying her brother-in-law John Dein and the Provost of Irvine. The ship did in fact sink, providing apparent material evidence for the charges. She was brought before local commissioners and subjected to torture.",
