    "gender": "Female",
    "biography": "Maria Holl, the wife of an innkeeper in the Imperial Free City of Nördlingen in Swabia, stands as one of the most remarkable individuals in the history of the European witch trials: a woman who survived sixty-two applications of torture without confessing, and was ultimately released. Her case represents an almost unique exception to the dynamic whereby torture inevitably produced the confession prosecutors sought, and her survival throws into stark relief the mechanism by which other accused did confess — not because their confessions were true, but because the pain was unbearable.\n\nHoll was accused during the Nördlingen witch panic, which ran intermittently from 1590 through the 1590s. She was brought before the town council and subjected to repeated torture sessions. Each time she refused to confess. The extraordinary number of torture applications — sixty-two — reflects the persistence of the prosecutors and the unusual determination of a woman who would not be broken. After sixty-two sessions, the Nördlingen town council, apparently doubting the guilt of a woman who could endure such treatment without confessing, released her.\n\nHoll's case is an extraordinary exception to the general pattern but also reveals the rule: the overwhelming majority of accused who faced even a fraction of her torture sessions confessed, not because they were guilty but because confession ended the unbearable pain. Her case shows that the torture system was not designed to ascertain truth but to produce confession, and that when an individual's psychological resistance was sufficient to withstand torture, the system had no mechanism to proceed — it could only release her or increase the torture further.",
    "arrest_narrative": "Holl was arrested during the Nördlingen witch panic of the 1590s following accusation by other accused. She was brought before the town council and subjected to prolonged judicial examination including repeated torture sessions.",
