    "biography": "Martha Corey had initially expressed skepticism about the witchcraft accusations sweeping Salem Village in early 1692, questioning whether the afflicted girls were really experiencing supernatural torment. This skepticism, voiced before she herself was accused, made her accusation particularly striking — it suggested that expressing doubt about the proceedings was itself dangerous. She was a full church member, which gave her some social respectability, and her husband Giles Corey was also accused, making theirs one of the few Salem cases involving an accused husband and wife.\n\nMartha Corey maintained her innocence throughout her trial and imprisonment. She was executed on September 22, 1692, in the last mass execution of the Salem trials, just three days after her husband Giles Corey had been pressed to death for refusing to enter a plea. Her case illustrates how skepticism about the proceedings could be reinterpreted as evidence of guilty knowledge.",
