    "biography": "Menichino della Nota was a peasant from Cividale del Friuli who identified himself as a benandante — one of those born with a caul (amniotic membrane) who claimed to leave their bodies in spirit form during the Ember Days to battle witches for the fertility of the crops. He was investigated by the Venetian Inquisition in 1618, decades after the earliest benandante cases, and his interrogation records form part of the corpus that Carlo Ginzburg analyzed in Night Battles (1966) and Ecstasies (1991).\n\nMenichino's confessions describe elaborate nocturnal battles in which the benandanti, armed with fennel stalks, fought against witches armed with sorghum stalks. The outcome of these battles determined whether the harvest would be good or poor that year. His account includes striking details about the spiritual journey — leaving his body behind while his spirit traveled, returning before dawn, and the exhaustion that followed. He insisted that the benandanti fought on behalf of the community for the common good, a claim that positioned him as a beneficial figure rather than a harmful one.\n\nGinzburg's analysis of Menichino and other benandanti cases argues that beneath the surface of these confessions lies evidence of a pre-Christian shamanistic complex in which specially marked individuals served as spiritual protectors of their communities through ecstatic battle. Over the course of multiple inquisitorial interrogations spanning decades, Ginzburg traced the gradual transformation of benandante self-identification toward the standard demonological framework of witch sabbath, as inquisitors' questions and expectations slowly reshaped the suspects' understanding of their own experiences.",
