    "biography": "Temperance Lloyd was an elderly, poor woman of Bideford in Devon whose execution on August 25, 1682, alongside Mary Trembles and Susanna Edwards, constitutes what are generally regarded as the last witch executions in England. She had a prior reputation in Bideford for witchcraft, having been previously accused and acquitted in 1671. Her re-accusation in 1682, when a local woman fell ill and attributed her condition to Lloyd's maleficium, led to her final prosecution.\n\nLloyd's case exemplifies the profile of the English accused identified by historians Alan Macfarlane and Keith Thomas: elderly, poor, socially marginalized, with a pre-existing community reputation for witchcraft that had survived one previous acquittal. She confessed to compact with the Devil and to causing the illness of her accuser. The three Bideford women were tried at the Exeter assizes and convicted, the last such conviction to result in execution in England.\n\nThe Bideford executions in 1682 attracted attention at the time as marking a cultural boundary: educated opinion was shifting decisively against belief in the reality of witchcraft, and the executions prompted criticism from some quarters. The publication of accounts of the Bideford case by the Reverend Francis Glanvill, defending the reality of witchcraft against growing skepticism, indicates that the executions were perceived as occurring in a context of genuine intellectual controversy about whether witchcraft was real.",
