    "biography": "Ursula Kemp was a midwife and healer in St Osyth, Essex, who provided services to local women in childbirth and illness. Her dual role as community healer and alleged source of maleficium was typical of the ambiguous social position occupied by cunning women: the same knowledge and reputation that made her useful could, under adverse circumstances, be interpreted as dangerous. She came to judicial attention through a complex web of neighborhood disputes and was prosecuted as part of the 1582 St Osyth witchcraft cases, one of the largest clusters of English witch prosecutions of the Elizabethan period.\n\nKemp's confession was elaborate and detailed, describing four familiar spirits with specific names and forms: two cats (Titty and Jack), a lamb (Tiffin), and a black toad (Piggin). Her confession also implicated numerous neighbors and became the central documentary source for the St Osyth prosecutions. Most dramatically, her own illegitimate son Thomas Rabbet testified against her — a detail contemporaries found particularly striking as evidence of the breakdown of natural bonds by witchcraft.",
