    "biography": "Walpurga Hausmennin was a licensed midwife in Dillingen an der Donau in Bavaria whose trial and execution in 1587 produced one of the most detailed and horrifying conviction records of the entire German witch persecution. She had practiced as a midwife for years, delivering the children of the community, before being accused of using her position to murder infants and dedicate them to the Devil. Her confession, extracted under torture, described decades of infant murder, weather magic, intercourse with a succession of demonic lovers, and attendance at sabbaths.\n\nThe accusations against Hausmennin follow a specific pattern that attached particular suspicion to midwives: they had access to newborn infants before baptism, they operated in the intimate domestic space of childbirth, and their role in managing complications of labor meant that infant deaths were part of their professional context. This structural position of vulnerability meant that when suspicion fell on a midwife, accusations of infant murder through witchcraft were the natural vehicle. Her confession attributed to her the murder of dozens of infants.\n\nThe execution of Walpurga Hausmennin was extraordinarily brutal even by the standards of the period. The sentence mandated that her flesh be torn with red-hot pincers in five places as she was conveyed through the streets, that her right hand (with which she had signed the diabolical pact) be cut off, that her body be burned, and that her ashes be thrown in the river. The detailed execution sentence has survived and been reproduced by historians as evidence of the performative cruelty of early modern judicial punishment.",
