    "biography": "Alison Peirson of Boarhills in Fife was tried and burned on May 28, 1588, in one of the most striking of the pre-North-Berwick Scottish witch cases. Her case is notable for several reasons: the high social status of her clients (including Patrick Adamson, Archbishop of St Andrews), the detailed testimony she gave about the fairy world, and the specific claim that her healing knowledge came from her dead cousin William Simpson, who now resided with the fairy folk.\n\nPeirson's description of receiving medical instruction from a dead kinsman who had become part of the fairy court is a variant of the pattern Bessie Dunlop described with Thomas Reid — the Scottish fairy tradition frequently involved the dead as intermediaries between the living and the fairy court, which in folk belief functioned partly as a realm of the dead. This suggests a coherent cultural system linking death, fairy, and healing knowledge rather than idiosyncratic personal invention.\n\nHer treatment of Archbishop Adamson — a high churchman who privately availed himself of her services — illustrates the market for cunning-folk healing that operated at all social levels in Scotland, even as official prosecutions intensified. Adamson's willingness to be treated by Peirson coexisted with an official religious position that should have condemned her practices, a tension typical of the complex relationship between elite disapproval and popular practice in this period.",
    "practice_description": "Alison Peirson practiced healing using knowledge she claimed to receive from the fairy folk and from her dead cousin William Simpson, who guided her from within the fairy court. She treated patients who came to her from the surrounding Fife countryside and beyond, including at least one high churchman. Her methods combined herbal remedies with the diagnostic framework of fairy-caused illness — she could identify whether sickness resulted from fairy interference and prescribe accordingly.",
