    "biography": "Anna la Rossa ('Anna the Red-Haired') is one of the female benandanti documented in Carlo Ginzburg's study of the Friulian inquisitorial records. Her presence in the corpus is significant because the benandante tradition was predominantly male in its documented form: most named practitioners who came before the Venetian Inquisition were men, and the primary role of combat against witches during Ember Days was typically gendered male. Female participation, when it occurred, complicates the picture.\n\nAnna's case suggests that women could claim membership in the benandante community and participate in the spirit journeys, though the specific roles assigned to female benandanti in these nocturnal encounters may have differed from those of their male counterparts. Her record survives in the inquisitorial archive of the Friuli, forming part of the larger corpus Ginzburg excavated when researching The Night Battles.\n\nThe 'la Rossa' epithet — a common Italian practice of appending a descriptive nickname — suggests she was identified within her community primarily by physical appearance, indicating she was a real, locally-known figure rather than a generic or composite character in inquisitorial records.",
    "practice_description": "Anna claimed participation in the benandante spirit journeys during the Ember Days, the four seasonal periods when benandanti believed their souls left their bodies to engage witches in ritual combat. Her specific role in these nocturnal encounters is less fully documented than those of male benandanti in the record, but she identified herself as part of the protective benandante community rather than as a witch.",
    "relationship_to_prosecution": "Investigated by the Venetian Inquisition as part of the broader inquiry into benandante activity in the Friuli. Her gender made her case complex for inquisitors: the benandante framework was predominantly male, while the witch-trial apparatus more commonly prosecuted women, creating interpretive tension in how her testimony was categorized.",
