    "title": "St. Vitus Dancing Plague: Mass Psychosomatic Event and Demonic Interpretation",
    "description": "In the summer of 1374, a mass outbreak of uncontrolled dancing seized thousands of people along the Rhine valley, particularly in Aachen and surrounding towns. Participants danced for days without rest, experiencing convulsions, hallucinations, and visions, many dying from exhaustion. The phenomenon spread through the Rhineland and recurred in subsequent decades, with notable outbreaks in Strasbourg (1518) and other locations. Contemporary observers described the dancers as bewitched or possessed, with the Church providing exorcisms and dedicating the phenomenon to the intercession of St. Vitus.\n\nThe demonological interpretation of the dancing plague illustrates the flexibility of the demonic framework: behaviors that modern observers would explain through mass psychogenic illness, ergot poisoning, or social stress could be readily assimilated into a theological vocabulary of possession and bewitchment. Religious and secular authorities responding to the outbreak treated it as a matter of spiritual intervention, reinforcing the practical reality of demonic power in the minds of contemporaries.\n\nThe dancing plagues also demonstrate how communities under social and economic stress — the 1374 outbreak followed the Black Death's repeated visitations — experienced collective suffering through frameworks that attributed causation to supernatural agents. This attribution structure is closely related to the logic that would later drive witchcraft accusation: an identifiable human agent must be responsible for collective misfortune, and that agent operates through demonic compact.",
