    "id": "timeline-1450-swiss-trials",
    "year": 1450,
    "year_end": 1460,
    "month": null,
    "day": null,
    "title": "Swiss and Savoy Witch Trials: Earliest Mass Prosecutions",
    "description": "The Swiss and Savoy regions witnessed some of the earliest well-documented mass witch prosecutions in European history during the mid-fifteenth century, representing a crucial transitional moment when the theoretical construction of the cumulative concept of witchcraft moved into judicial practice. These trials, concentrated in the Alpine regions, systematically combined elements that had previously existed separately in law and theology: the maleficium (harmful magic), the diabolical pact, flight to nocturnal assemblies, and participation in the sabbath. The prosecutions drew on the theoretical groundwork laid by earlier inquisitors and jurists, particularly in the Dauphiné and Savoy, where secular and ecclesiastical courts cooperated in unprecedented ways to pursue what they understood as an organized heretical sect.\n\nThe Swiss trials of this period are particularly significant because they produced detailed confessional records describing sabbath activities that would become standardized across European demonological literature. Accused witches described flight to mountain gatherings, feasts, dancing, and sexual intercourse with demons — elements that appeared in Nider's Formicarius (1435) and would be systematized in the Malleus Maleficarum three decades later. The Alpine geography, with its isolated communities and strong beliefs in mountain spirits and nocturnal gatherings, appears to have provided fertile ground for the fusion of learned demonological theory with local folk practice.\n\nHistorians have debated the extent to which these early prosecutions represent a genuine cumulation of pre-existing beliefs versus an imposition of elite theological frameworks onto popular practice. Richard Kieckhefer's analysis emphasizes how the cumulative concept of witchcraft — the idea that maleficent magic necessarily implied diabolical pact and sabbath attendance — was constructed through a series of intellectual steps between roughly 1370 and 1450, with the Swiss trials representing its first systematic judicial application.",
    "event_type": "TRIAL",
    "significance": "HIGH",
    "region": "Switzerland/Savoy",
