    "month": null,
    "day": null,
    "title": "Scottish Witchcraft Act 1563",
    "description": "The Scottish Witchcraft Act of 1563 was passed during the regency period of Mary Queen of Scots and made witchcraft a capital offense in Scotland for the first time. The act was part of a broader program of Protestant Reformation legislation passed by the Scottish Parliament in 1563 and reflected the influence of Calvinist reformers, particularly John Knox, who sought to purify Scotland of both Catholic and magical practices. Unlike the contemporary English legislation, the Scottish act explicitly criminalized not only the practice of witchcraft but also consulting with witches, making both the practitioner and the client liable for capital punishment. This broader scope reflected Reformed Protestant theology's concern with idolatry and improper religious practice in all its forms.\n\nThe act created the legal framework that enabled Scotland's relatively intense witch prosecution over the following century and a half. Scotland's execution rate per capita was among the highest in Europe — estimates suggest perhaps 1,500 executions, compared to England's 300-400, in a country with roughly a fifth of England's population. The reasons for this disparity have been extensively debated: Christina Larner's foundational work emphasized the role of the centralized Scottish church courts and the personal involvement of James VI in promoting prosecution, while later historians including Julian Goodare have pointed to the structure of Scottish secular courts and the role of local elites in driving accusation.\n\nThe 1563 act remained in force until 1735 when it was replaced by a new act that decriminalized witchcraft as such. During its century and a half of operation, it served as the legal foundation for prosecutions including the North Berwick trials (1590), the Auldearn trials involving Isobel Gowdie (1662), and hundreds of smaller local prosecutions across Scotland's regions. The act's broad scope — covering consulting with witches as well as practicing witchcraft — helps explain why Scottish accusation patterns sometimes differed from English ones.",
