    "month": null,
    "day": null,
    "title": "Basque Witch Panic and Salazar Enriquez's Skeptical Investigation",
    "description": "A major witch panic erupted in the Basque region straddling the Spanish-French border in 1609-1610, triggered in part by the simultaneous panic and executions occurring on the French side at Labourd under judge Pierre de Lancre. In Spain, the Inquisition dispatched Alonso de Salazar y Frías to investigate and his meticulous examination of evidence led him to conclude that there was no substantial evidence that any of the alleged witchcraft had actually occurred. Salazar examined thousands of cases, tested alleged witches' ointments and powders, and found no physical evidence supporting the claims made in confessions. His 1612 report to the Spanish Inquisition effectively halted witch prosecution in Spain, with the Inquisition's Suprema issuing instructions in 1614 requiring much more careful evidentiary standards before witchcraft prosecutions could proceed.\n\nThe contrast between the French and Spanish responses to the same Basque panic is historiographically significant. On the French side, de Lancre's prosecutions resulted in some 80 executions and a published account, Tableau de l'inconstance des mauvais anges (1612), that is a major demonological text. On the Spanish side, Salazar's empirical investigation produced a skeptical report that constrained further prosecution. This contrast has been used by historians to analyze institutional factors in witch prosecution: the Spanish Inquisition's more hierarchical and bureaucratic structure, with its tradition of skeptical oversight, may have been more resistant to local panics than French secular courts.\n\nGustav Henningsen's study The Witches' Advocate (1980) is the definitive scholarly work on Salazar's investigation, drawing on the inquisitorial archives to reconstruct his methodology and conclusions in detail. Henningsen treats Salazar as an early empiricist who applied something resembling evidentiary standards to claims that most contemporaries accepted uncritically.",
    "event_type": "TRIAL",
    "significance": "HIGH",
    "region": "Spain/France",
